Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and how we will use them  
• `borough`:   filter the dataset to the borough we care about ( `'Barking and Dagenham'` )  
• `major_category`:   the high‑level crime type we need to rank  
• `value`:   the monthly incident count we must aggregate (SUM)  
• `year`, `month`:   optional – can be used for time‑series exploration once the data are filtered  
(Columns such as `lsoa_code` and `minor_category` are not needed for the requested ranking.)

Main filter condition  
```sql
WHERE borough = 'Barking and Dagenham'
```

Below are ten progressively richer exploratory queries (each capped with `LIMIT 20`) culminating in the final query that answers the task.

```sql
-- 1. List a sample of all distinct borough names
SELECT DISTINCT borough
FROM `bigquery-public-data.london_crime.crime_by_lsoa`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Inspect a handful of raw crime records for Barking and Dagenham
SELECT *
FROM `bigquery-public-data.london_crime.crime_by_lsoa`
WHERE borough = 'Barking and Dagenham'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Show the distinct major crime categories recorded for this borough
SELECT DISTINCT major_category
FROM `bigquery-public-data.london_crime.crime_by_lsoa`
WHERE borough = 'Barking and Dagenham'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Verify that the monthly values are integers and get a feel for their range
SELECT value
FROM `bigquery-public-data.london_crime.crime_by_lsoa`
WHERE borough = 'Barking and Dagenham'
ORDER BY value DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Aggregate total incidents (all categories combined) per year
SELECT year,
       SUM(value) AS total_incidents
FROM `bigquery-public-data.london_crime.crime_by_lsoa`
WHERE borough = 'Barking and Dagenham'
GROUP BY year
ORDER BY year
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Aggregate incidents per major category (unsorted preview)
SELECT major_category,
       SUM(value) AS incidents
FROM `bigquery-public-data.london_crime.crime_by_lsoa`
WHERE borough = 'Barking and Dagenham'
GROUP BY major_category
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Same as #6 but ordered to see which categories dominate
SELECT major_category,
       SUM(value) AS incidents
FROM `bigquery-public-data.london_crime.crime_by_lsoa`
WHERE borough = 'Barking and Dagenham'
GROUP BY major_category
ORDER BY incidents DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Break down incidents by year AND major category (time trend)
SELECT year,
       major_category,
       SUM(value) AS incidents
FROM `bigquery-public-data.london_crime.crime_by_lsoa`
WHERE borough = 'Barking and Dagenham'
GROUP BY year, major_category
ORDER BY year, incidents DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Zoom in on the top major category (from query #7) and list its minor categories
SELECT minor_category,
       SUM(value) AS incidents
FROM `bigquery-public-data.london_crime.crime_by_lsoa`
WHERE borough = 'Barking and Dagenham'
  AND major_category = (
        SELECT major_category
        FROM `bigquery-public-data.london_crime.crime_by_lsoa`
        WHERE borough = 'Barking and Dagenham'
        GROUP BY major_category
        ORDER BY SUM(value) DESC
        LIMIT 1
      )
GROUP BY minor_category
ORDER BY incidents DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL ANSWER: Top three major crime categories in Barking and Dagenham
SELECT major_category,
       SUM(value) AS incidents
FROM `bigquery-public-data.london_crime.crime_by_lsoa`
WHERE borough = 'Barking and Dagenham'
GROUP BY major_category
ORDER BY incidents DESC
LIMIT 3;
```

Running the final query (#10) will return exactly the three major crime categories that have registered the highest number of incidents in Barking and Dagenham together with their aggregated counts.
